{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793458371,"nonce":"uA7FzSYfAFbRZpfAtXS0Im618dQ=","title":"Blocking std primitives hold their scheduler thread; park only the calling fiber","message":"Requested by the RayDE session. A native call that blocks (process stdout/stderr read, stdin write, exit wait, Sys.stdin read, socket reads) holds up every fiber on the same scheduler thread. hl_blocking (be17f1bd, git-bug 44ad2d9) lets the collector proceed but does not free the thread.\n\nLayout today: the main fiber runs on the main scheduler; compiled Thread.create bodies go round-robin to a worker pool of cores-1 threads (ASH_WORKERS); pure-interpreter bodies stay on the main scheduler. So two blocked readers can share a worker with other compiled threads, and in --mode interp every Haxe thread shares one OS thread (the known fiber blocking-IO deadlock).\n\nDesign: the future carrier already parks only the calling fiber (hlp_future_await, rt::Waiter) and is completed from a foreign OS thread. Add an offload helper that runs a blocking closure on a small pool of OS threads, parks the calling fiber, and wakes it on completion; read into a runtime-owned buffer and copy into the Haxe bytes on wake. Apply to process stdout/stderr read, stdin write, exit(block), Sys.stdin read first, then sockets (timeouts, select) and any other blocking primitive found. This also lets --mode interp run such programs.\n\nAcceptance: a reader parked on an idle child does not delay a sibling fiber on the same worker, in hybrid, jit and interp; cancellation or process kill unblocks the read; no copy of GC-owned bytes is touched off-thread.","files":null}]}